[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) says its reports are one issue with one fix queued. They are not. Read by
tenant and by body, its reports are three problems, and the queued parser fix closes only the
first one.

<Callout kind="warn">
Not one issue. Three problems, each with its own ticket. The queued fix does not close #2 or #3.
</Callout>

## What #4471 holds

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} />

- **parser.** The shared parser reads every date month first, and every tenant here writes the day
  first. Day and month swap: 7 April becomes 4 July. This is the bug #4471 names.
- **hint.** booking-v3's form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field, so people type the month
  first, and the form reads the value day first. A correct parser still gets these wrong. Only
  tenants on booking-v3 who book through the form see it.
- **shift.** The date moves by one day, Tuesday to Monday, and the day and month do not swap. One
  meeting reached Lisbon as Tuesday and Toronto as Monday. This is time-zone handling. The desk
  already read these as a one-day move (`desk-notes.json`), but the #4471 page still counts them
  under `parser`.

## How the counts are made

- Reports are counted by id. The ticket export overlaps, so some reports appear twice.
- A report's problem comes from its tenant's locale and booking form, plus the desk's notes on the
  body. Intake's tag is a guess from the reporter's browser. It misfiles reports both ways, so
  nothing here counts by it.
- Nothing is typed in. The table is rebuilt from the export on every build, so new reports land in
  the right row. A report no rule places shows up as `unplaced` with no ticket.

## Since April

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

## Lessons

- Matching on subject merged three problems into one ticket ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)).
  The subject says what a person saw. The cause comes from their tenant and from what the body says.
- Before you mark a big ticket known, check whether one fix closes every report in it.

